course: dictionary
lesson: 4135
title: Gloat: smug and unkind
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When you win, you can feel happy, but to gloat is to show it in a smug, unkind way that rubs it in for other people. Gloating is not about feeling proud. It is about making someone else feel small. Look at the clues in the sentence: «And Ruby, beaten by a gloating boy, simply said good game.»
explain: Ruby lost, and the boy was pleased about it. The word beaten tells you Ruby lost, so the boy was pleased about her loss. Ruby was kind and simply said good game. Gloating is the opposite of that kind of answer, so the boy was being unkind.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "The boy showed smug pleasure about beating Ruby" still makes sense, so gloating must mean showing smug, unkind pleasure. Remember that gloat has a friend in the word smug, which means pleased with yourself in an annoying way.
fact: Gloat is a verb: gloat, gloats, gloated, gloating.
fact: «Gloat» means to show you are very pleased about your own success, or about someone else's bad luck, in a smug and unkind way.
fact: You can gloat over something or gloat about something, but the feeling is always smug and unkind.
fact: In sport, gloating is bad manners: a good winner does not gloat.
fact: In stories, a villain who gloats tells the reader that they are cruel and proud.
exercise: «And Ruby, beaten by a gloating boy, simply said good game.» What is the boy doing? | *Showing smug pleasure about winning | Shaking hands with the referee | Saying sorry to Ruby | Crying after losing
exercise: In a story, what does it tell the reader when a villain gloats? | They are very tired | *They are cruel and proud | They are shy and kind | They have lost their voice
exercise: Which is the best way to be a good winner? | *Shake hands and do not gloat | Tell everyone the other team cheated | Rub it in about the other team's bad luck | Shout about the win over everyone
test: Nobody likes a winner who keeps on showing off. Which word fits the gap? Nobody likes a winner who will ___ over the losers. | yawn | whisper | sigh | *gloat
test: What does it mean to gloat about someone's bad luck? | To feel sorry for them | To help them up | To forget it quickly | *To show smug pleasure in their bad luck
exam: Which sentence uses gloat in the right way? | Ruby gloated and then helped her friend up | The teacher gloated when the class worked hard | *The boy gloated over his win and laughed at the losers | Dad gloated quietly about the lovely picnic
